El jueves, 17 de junio de 2021 a las 1:49:12 UTC+2, João Gonçalves escribió:
> Hi,
> Álvaro it seems the problem is on the data side more specifically in the input segmented raster which is not properly formated. I corrected it and the classification runs well with average test kappa ~0.7
>
> - First, the segmented raster should be an integer (floating precision is OK but it takes more space);
>
> - Second, image segments must have a unique identifier number (integer) assigned to each one. In your case, the data in clip_segmentation_2016.tif identifies 'only' 200 unique segments when it is clear that the segmented image has much more. In this situation, you need to run a region grouping tool (with four/rook-rule or eight/queen-rule connections) to assign each segment a unique integer ID - check this  <https://gis.stackexchange.com/questions/244849/a-tool-in-qgis-like-region-group>or this <https://desktop.arcgis.com/en/arcmap/10.5/tools/spatial-analyst-toolbox/region-group.htm> tool to do that.
>
> Also, check your image segmentation workflow to verify if some step is missing in your pipeline. Because you are running multiple images verify if all segmented rasters are properly formated with unique IDs for each segment.
>
> Will send you an email with the raster grouped data with unique IDs so you can check it.
>
>
> Cheers
> João
